    T47
        title       Fixture infrastructure and pilot parts: shapes, scripts, capability lists, gates (slice R)
        status      open
        priority    high
        blocked_by  [@T51]
        description
            Build the fixture infrastructure and pilot it on three composite parts: `shape()` modules as data, scripts
            as JSON, a script player, runtime capability lists, the reach recorder, the feature matrix with the
            combination gate, and the `bench` runner (slice R).

            **Why now:** about 70 of 112 hand cases use one application (checkout Pay); the user wants a wide net of
            flows, state transitions and network faults, written once and read by the generator, the Spec model and the
            benchmark replay (2026-11-11). Composites run on production and the model only; round-14 stays a mutant host
            for the 99 hand cases, which freeze.

            **Read first:** @task.V3.T45, code:workflow/plans/flow/references/proposals/fixture-catalogue-2026-11-11.md
            (sections 2, 4, 5A, 5B and 7), code:workflow/plans/flow/references/proposals/replan-2026-11-11.md (part 3,
            "R Rework"), code:packages/flow-state-v3/conformance/world/network.ts,
            @spec.Conformance.R15 to R23 (tests T30, T31, T33 to T36, T39, T44 are this Task's),
            code:workflow/plans/flow/references/proposals/plan-task-coverage-2026-11-11.md (the 265 features).

            **Builds on T45:** the script Schema, player and shrinker are @task.V3.T45's; this Task adds parts and the
            scripts generated from them, and writes no second set.

            **Risk:** the interacting-pair count is an estimate (671 by the replan's reading, 777 by the catalogue's).
            The reach recorder measures it, and the user approves the non-interacting rules once, as one list (replan
            question 12).

            **Approach, progressive fixtures:** fixtures grow with the slices, so no part waits on a missing runtime
            feature. Each part declares the feature codes it needs. Each runtime (production, the Spec model, round-14)
            declares the codes it implements. The generator enables only operations whose codes the runtime has. The
            feature-matrix gate reads a feature with no runtime as "owed by slice N" and fails one that a runtime
            implements but no fixture covers. The combination gate counts only pairs among implemented codes. A part
            lands in the slice that builds its first feature and gains operations as later slices land theirs.

            **Approach, build:** one `shape()` module per part, with its reference derived from production's `Compiled`,
            a user model and a server model; usual and stress profiles; the app mix; the matrix maps all 265 Plan
            features, each row naming its fixture kind (composite part, hand case, static case, example-app check or 
            package
            check). Pilot parts: S16 into C, S21 flattened into H, S10 into I (39 interacting pairs, an estimate).
            Composites A to K are placed by slice in the replan's part tables; J and K (catalogue 5B) are built in slice
            3 with the subset available. S27 and the Plan-check shapes arrive with their slices. Effort 4 (estimate).

            **Catalogue correction:** the catalogue's section 1 claims (production writes under four policies and has
            `cancelAll`) and its P tiers (section 7: 11 shapes run on production now) are wrong for production today
            (replan part 1). The catalogue file is not edited; this Task's matrix and the slice tables supersede them.

            **Declares today's codes:** the 15 codes production implements now (the replan's R row), listed from
            `adapters/production.ts` and recorded in STATUS.md, T47.

            **Bench:** turn cost per part (median and p99) is recorded as a measurement, not a gate; only the work
            fingerprint check gates (the user declined up-front optimisation, 2026-11-11).

            **Order:** one lead at a time: T45, then T51, then T47; in every slice the suite half finishes before the
            production half, by the same builder or the next.

            - **May change:** `packages/flow-state-v3/conformance/` (fixtures, world, generator, runner).
            - **Evidence:** STATUS.md, one T47 section; the generated gate file.
            - **False pass:** parts that differ in name but reach the same cells → caught by table-cell reach and model
              mutation across parts.
            - **Gates:** matrix, combination, falsify, liar and core-mutant gates; `nub run check`.
            - **Stops:** the user's approval of the non-interacting pair list.
        acceptance
            - `shape()` modules, JSON scripts and the script player exist: a script generated from a part replays under
              the oracles on production, and the Spec model computes its expected reports.
            - each fixture part declares the feature codes it needs, and each runtime (production, the Spec model,
              round-14) declares the codes it implements.
            - the generator enables only operations whose codes the runtime has.
            - the feature-matrix gate treats a feature with no runtime as "owed by slice N", fails a feature a runtime
              implements that is in no fixture, and maps all 265 Plan features with the fixture kind per row.
            - the combination gate counts only pairs among implemented codes, takes the interacting-pair list from the
              channel table, fails an unexercised pair with no dated waiver, and reports triples and cell-pair reach.
            - the pilot parts S16 [C], S21 flattened [H] and S10 [I] each have usual and stress profiles, kill
              table-cell mutants of the model, and run on production, alone and in the app mix.
            - the `bench` runner replays the same scripts without oracles and reproduces the work fingerprint; turn cost
              per part is recorded (median and p99) as a measurement, not a gate; the fingerprint check is a gate.
            - every failure found shrinks to a kept regression case or is logged as a runtime limit.
            - Declared: the capability lists exist and today's implemented codes are declared in each.
            - Enabled: S16, S21 and S10 declare their needs and the generator draws only declared operations.
            - Covered: the combination gate passes over the pairs among today's implemented codes.
            - Guards: production's first-contact reds are recorded and classified, held-out seeds pass, and a sample of
              the model's expectations is read by a different model family (Fable).
            - the non-interacting pair list is generated from the channel table, and the user approves its rules once,
              as one list.
            - the Compile static cases run on every composite's `Compiled`.
